.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess \- store and read secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ess
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ess
module is euclid's secrets store: the passwords, connection strings and
tokens the things euclid runs need in order to reach anything else.
.PP
It exists because those otherwise live in a configuration file, an
environment variable or a deployment script \- readable by everyone who
can read the host, copied into every backup, and impossible to rotate
without a release. A secret here is written once, read by name, and
rotated in place while the things using it keep running.
.SH ENCRYPTION
Every value is encrypted before it is stored, under a key held by
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
and decrypted only by
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1).
There is no path through this module that writes a value in the clear.
That split is the point: the key management module keeps the key, the
secrets store keeps the ciphertext, and a copy of either alone \- a
database dump, a backup, a mongo shell \- is worth nothing.
.PP
A secret records which key it was written under, so re-keying one
secret leaves every other alone and a value stored years ago is still
read back with the key it was actually written with. Naming another key
on
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1)
re-encrypts it, which is how a secret is moved off a key that is being
retired.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-key
a secret is encrypted under the namespace's own secrets key, which the
module creates the first time something needs one \- named
.I ess
for an unscoped namespace and
.RI \fI ess-<namespace> \fR
otherwise. It is an ordinary EKM key: it appears in
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1),
and it is the thing to guard.
.SH WHAT IS AND IS NOT SECRET
The value is. The name, the description, the tags and the dates are
not: they are what a caller lists, searches and audits by, and they are
readable by anyone who may list secrets. Nothing that belongs in the
value should be put in a description.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1)
never carries a value, and neither does the answer to a create or a
rotation \- a caller that has just written one already has it, and an
answer that echoed it would put it in a log and a terminal for no
reason.
.PP
Every read is logged by the server: who read what, and when. The value
is not part of that and never appears in a log line.
.SH WHO CAN READ WHAT
Every
.B ess
action requires an authenticated caller. Beyond that, a principal
deployed with particular resources \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
under
.B IDENTITY
\- reaches exactly the secrets it was given and no others, and a
listing shows it only those. That is what lets an application hold the
one password it needs rather than the account's whole store, and it
matters more here than elsewhere: a grant on a queue bounds what can be
changed, a grant on a secret bounds what can be read.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-secret
Stores a new secret, encrypted. See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1).
.TP
.B get-secret
Reads a secret's value. The only action that decrypts one. See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1).
.TP
.B list-secrets
Lists secrets with their descriptions, versions and ages, never their
values. See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1).
.TP
.B update-secret
Rotates a secret, changes its description, or moves it to another key.
See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1).
.TP
.B delete-secret
Deletes a secret and the value it holds. See
.BR euclid-cli-ess-delete-secret (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I ess
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B ess
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials ,
and signed with RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures using the stored
access key if there is one.
.SH EVENTS
A create publishes
.IR ess.secret.created ,
a rotation
.IR ess.secret.rotated ,
and a delete
.IR ess.secret.deleted ,
carrying the name and version but never the value \- so an application
subscribed through
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
can reload a password when it changes rather than holding the one it
started with.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Store a password without it ever reaching the shell history, read it
back into an environment variable, then rotate it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ess create-secret --name db-password --value-stdin \e
    --description "production postgres"

export PGPASSWORD=$(euclid-cli ess get-secret -n db-password --raw)

openssl rand -base64 24 | \e
    euclid-cli ess update-secret --name db-password --value-stdin
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-update-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-delete-secret (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
